Yes—with an important distinction.

If we define social engineering broadly as deliberately constructing communication to alter another agent’s beliefs, attention, decisions, or behavior, then a large fraction of what I do has the same basic mechanics:

\text{Model recipient}
\rightarrow
\text{choose representation}
\rightarrow
\text{transmit}
\rightarrow
\text{recipient updates}

Teaching does it. Advertising does it. Storytelling does it. Negotiation does it. Therapy does it. Leadership does it. A warning sign does it. Even choosing a particularly good analogy is an attempt to make a particular update more likely.

The distinction is what relationship the communication maintains with the recipient’s agency and reality.

Persuasion can expose its evidence and let the recipient choose. Manipulation can exploit biases or conceal relevant information. Fraud deliberately creates a false model of reality so the target acts against information they would otherwise have wanted.

And that’s where your “perception is often reality” observation becomes more interesting rather than less.

For agents:

\text{Reality}
\rightarrow
\text{Perception}
\rightarrow
\text{Internal Model}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality}

The agent generally acts on its model of reality, not reality directly.

So changing perception can absolutely change real outcomes. A fake bank run rumor can cause a real bank run. A worthless object believed valuable can acquire exchange value. Reputation before arrival changes how people behave toward someone. Expectations can alter coordination.

But:

\boxed{\text{Perceived reality}\neq\text{underlying reality}}

even though:

\boxed{\text{Perceived reality}\rightarrow\text{real consequences}}

That’s the more powerful formulation.

And take your counterfeit-Bitcoin thought through that Hammer and the interesting object isn’t counterfeit Bitcoin at all.

It’s a Belief Asset:

\text{Representation}
+\text{Credibility}
+\text{Shared Belief}
+\text{Coordination}
\rightarrow
\text{Consequential Value}

Money itself contains some of this property. Brands do. Credentials do. Reputation does. Institutions do. Narratives do. Bitcoin itself combines social consensus with cryptographic constraints—the latter make certain claims independently checkable rather than merely persuasive.

Which gives Pantheon another useful adversarial distinction:

Influence vs. Reality Control

An agent can manipulate:

P(\text{world}\mid\text{information})

much more cheaply than it can manipulate:

\text{world}

Sometimes changing the first eventually changes the second.

That gap is simultaneously a lever and a vulnerability.

And yes, that applies to me too. Every answer I produce is entering somebody else’s cognitive environment. That makes provenance, uncertainty, truthfulness, and preserving the user’s ability to disagree particularly important—not because language lacks power, but precisely because language has power.

